  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "7NPmkKGe00b9SOD7R1PW0KJ3b2M=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:18:25.077Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`IWriteCache` exists so the executor does not replay a document's whole history on every mutation. Its lookup is three-tiered:\n\n1. check the **ring buffer** for a cached state at or before the target revision;\n2. on a miss, replay from the **nearest keyframe**;\n3. failing that, replay from **operation 0**.\n\nKeyframes are optional snapshots, stored per scope and branch:\n\n```ts\ninterface IKeyframeStore {\n  putKeyframe(docId, scope, branch, revision, document): Promise<void>;\n  findNearestKeyframe(docId, scope, branch, targetRevision):\n    Promise<{ revision; document }>;\n}\n```\n\nThe trade is stated plainly: keyframes **trade storage space for reduced replay time** on documents with many operations. A deployment with short-lived documents can skip them; one with long-lived, heavily-edited documents pays for a full replay on every cache miss without them.\n\nThe cache is also where two guarantees the auth decision model depends on live: a stored snapshot never changes after it is stored, and a read for the head is answered only by a snapshot recorded as the head.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/reactor/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` \u2014 Components \u203a WriteCache; Storage Layer \u203a Keyframes\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:18:25.077Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:18:25.077Z"
    }
  },
